  "statements": {
    "static-friction-magnitude": "Static friction can take any magnitude from zero up to a maximum, and the maximum is the coefficient of static friction times the normal force. The friction is whatever it needs to be to keep the surfaces at rest, until the applied force passes that maximum and the object moves.",
    "kinetic-friction-magnitude": "Once an object is moving, the friction on it is the coefficient of kinetic friction times the normal force. Friction that obeys this rule is said to behave simply, and its magnitude does not depend on the area in contact or, to a good approximation, on the speed.",
    "friction-on-an-incline": "On a slope, the normal force is not the whole weight but only its component perpendicular to the surface, so the kinetic friction is the coefficient times mg cos θ. An object that slides down the slope at constant speed therefore has a coefficient of kinetic friction equal to tan θ."
  },
